Z||000007||Jay||05-07-2001||07:23 PM||||I believe one of the fixes is a heat shield and different routing for various lines.  Correct me if I'm wrong but I haven't had an 84 in almost 10 years.  You could check any cars this way, not jsut Fieros.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||PBJ||05-07-2001||07:33 PM||||The most important and noticable signs of the recall done on 84's is a large heat shield bolted between exhaust maifold and head/block. Also the wiring harness will now be wraped in a "metal" tape.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Nashco||05-07-2001||11:46 PM||||OH MY GOODNESS! Ok, this is a very touchy issue with me right now, so I'll try to keep it short.<P>If you call in your VIN, Pontiac will tell you the recall has been performed, regardless of whether it has or not.  This is because Pontiac purged all the Fiero information from the computer two years ago.<P>As far as what signs to look for to know whether the recall has been done, there are several.  The easiest ones to look for are the dipstick and the sticker.  Here's a link to <A HREF="http://www.michiganfieroclub.org/#recalls" TARGET=_blank>Michigan Fiero Club's Ultimate Recall Page</A> that contains all the info you need to know.  This will tell you what was supposed to be done, so you can look very closely for it all.<P>I went through an ENORMOUS hassle trying to get my recall performed on my 88 GT, mostly due to lack of cooperation from the local service manager.  I highly recommend you get this work done, whether your car runs or not.  If it's not been done, you're still entitled to it, and it will be that many less things you'll have to worry about breaking or needing replaced in the future.<P>As you'll see, there's an easy 18-20 hours available for the dealership if they need the work, so don't be afraid to take it down there.  If you have any problems, call 1-800-PMCARES and let them know you haven't had the work performed, and would like to have a visual inspection.<P>One word of the wise about PMCARES...if you get somebody that isn't VERY helpful, don't talk to them anymore, just talk to the person who answers the call.  Once you find somebody that is courteous and helpful, always refer to their name and request they call you.  I found that the representative on the phone had 1000% more leverage than me and could make or break the recall issue.<P>Well, this should help.  If you want to talk to me about it, I'm more than willing to help.  I found out a lot, because I had to jump through so many hoops (due to a-hole service manager).  Send me an email if you want more info or have questions.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||05-08-2001||08:12 PM||||Links to all the U.S. recalls in my cave. There's one V6 one there that Michigan doesn't have.<P><A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A>||||reg||1
